honors without a pair, but they are dragons which can give Dragon Pung points if I get a pung later. However, they are less flexible for forming chows.  \n- Discard T1: Isolated terminal, hard to make chow, but keeping it might help with Pung of Terminals or Honors (1 point) if I draw more.  \n- Discard F4: Single wind (North, prevailing wind), could be valuable if I draw another, but currently only one.  \n- Discard B3 or B9: I have pairs of these, could be kept to potentially form pungs, but they are suited tiles and might be needed for chows if I shift strategy.  \n\nReason: Since I have multiple pairs, Seven Pairs is a viable pattern worth 24 points, which is high and achievable. For Seven Pairs, I need seven different pairs. Currently, I have pairs: W2, W7, B3, B9, F3 (that's 5 pairs). Plus singles: T1, F4, J1, J2 (4 singles). I need to turn two of these singles into pairs and get one more pair from somewhere. So I should keep tiles that have higher chance to pair. Unshown counts: F4 has 4 left, J1 has 2 left, J2 has 1 left, T1 has 2 left. F4 has the most remaining, so keeping F4 might be better. J2 has only 1 left, so it's unlikely to pair. T1 has 2 left, moderate chance. Thus, discard J2 or J1. J2 has lower remaining count (1) than J1 (2), so J2 is less likely to pair. Also, discarding a dragon might reduce chance for Two Dragon Pungs, but Seven Pairs is higher priority. Additionally, discarding J2 might be safer since across player played J2 twice in history, so they might not need it.  \n\nI choose to discard J2 to move towards Seven Pairs while retaining tiles with higher pairing potential.\n\nAnswer: Play J2"}
